    <tags>exosnap recorder screen-capture screen-recorder nvenc h264 hevc av1 hdr mkv mp4 webm webcam diagnostics</tags>
    <summary>Windows-native screen, window and region recorder with an NVIDIA NVENC pipeline, multi-track audio, and diagnostics.</summary>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/Exoridus/exosnap/releases/tag/v0.9.0</releaseNotes>
    <description><![CDATA[
ExoSnap is a Windows-native screen, window and region recorder built around an
NVIDIA NVENC capture-and-encode pipeline, multi-track audio routing, a webcam
overlay, and a diagnostics-first workflow that blocks a misconfigured recording
before it starts.

This package downloads and installs the official ExoSnap 0.9.0 MSI from the
project's GitHub Release. No binaries are redistributed inside the package.

## Features

* Capture a monitor (DXGI Output Duplication), an application window (Windows
  Graphics Capture) or a screen region, at a constant 60 fps by default.
* NVIDIA NVENC video encoding: H.264, HEVC and AV1, with 10-bit (P010) output
  for HEVC Main10 and AV1.
* An HDR desktop records as tone-mapped SDR (BT.709) by default; an expert
  setting switches to native HDR10 (PQ / BT.2020, 10-bit) with HEVC or AV1.
* Multi-track audio - application, system and microphone sources, each on its
  own track by default, any source mergeable onto the track above it.
* Containers: MKV (default), WebM and MP4. Audio: AAC-LC, Opus, and lossless
  PCM and FLAC (PCM and FLAC are MKV-only).
* Webcam picture-in-picture overlay, TOML recording presets with import and
  export, rebindable global hotkeys, and single-frame capture.
* Built-in editor for keyframe-accurate lossless trimming and markers, exported
  by stream copy.
* Pre-flight readiness gate, live pipeline monitoring (frame drops, A/V drift,
  disk-fill estimate) and a post-flight report card.
* Crash recovery: an interrupted recording is offered for Finish, Continue or
  Delete on the next launch.
* Opt-in in-app updates on a Stable or Preview channel, hash- and
  signature-verified, with every install step visible - never a silent
  auto-install.

## Requirements

* Windows 10 or 11, x64. Windows 11 is the primary target, Windows 10 is
  best-effort.
* An NVIDIA GPU with NVENC support (RTX 20-series or newer recommended) and a
  current NVIDIA display driver. AMD, Intel and software (CPU) encoding are not
  available in this release.
* The Microsoft Visual C++ 2015-2022 x64 runtime, which Chocolatey installs
  automatically through this package's `vcredist140` dependency.

## Notes

* ExoSnap is free and open-source software under the GNU GPL, version 3 or
  later. It is not trial software and requires no license key.
* This is a pre-1.0 preview. Settings, preset and recording-history file
  formats are not frozen and may change incompatibly before 1.0.
* The published MSI is not code-signed yet, so Windows SmartScreen may warn on
  first launch.
* The HEVC, hvc1 and 10-bit encoder paths work end to end but have not been
  validated across every NVIDIA GPU generation. Use H.264 or AV1 if you hit a
  problem.
]]></description>
    <!-- exosnap.exe and the shipped Qt6 DLLs link the dynamic MSVC runtime (/MD);
         the MSI does not bundle vcruntime140/msvcp140. Without this dependency a
         clean machine fails to launch with STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND (0xC0000135). -->
    <dependencies>
      <!-- The redistributable must be at least as new as the MSVC toolset that
           built the release binaries: a newer toolset may emit calls to runtime
           exports an older redistributable does not have. Release MSIs are built
           on the GitHub Actions windows-2022 image (VS 2022 17.14, MSVC toolset
           14.44), so 14.44 is the floor rather than the VS 2022 baseline.
           Re-check whenever the release runner image moves to a newer toolset. -->
      <dependency id="vcredist140" version="14.44.35211" />
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
